\chapter{Conditionals, state, and returns}
\label{ch:16}
\index{Conditionals, state, and returns}

A conditional evaluates its condition and transfers control into the chosen block. Lexical bindings made inside a branch are available only inside that scope. An assignment updates an existing mutable local of the appropriate type. An explicit \OnxCodeTerm{return} exits the function and participates in the checked cleanup sequence. Source short-circuit decisions must preserve which branch or operand executes after lowering.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn choose(flag: bool, n: i64) -> i64
{
  var answer = n;
  if flag { answer = answer + 1; }
  else { answer = answer - 1; }
  return answer;
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

To verify branch behavior, call the helper on the same small input with both flags and assert distinct results. Add a case that would trap if the inactive branch were evaluated. In checked arithmetic, running an extra operation can change observable behavior even if its value is unused. MIR represents scalar control through explicit edges and block arguments, covered in \cref{ch:79}.

\section{Find the state that reaches the return}
Draw a fork after the initialization of \OnxCodeTerm{answer} and write
10 beside the fork as the incoming value. Along the true edge the
assignment reads 10, computes 11, and writes it back. Along the false
edge the read also sees 10, because the true edge was not traversed;
the resulting value is 9. The return reads the value arriving on the
chosen edge. One mistake is to trace both assignments top to bottom,
getting 10. Another is to replace an assignment with an inner
\OnxCodeTerm{let answer}: that introduces a new binding and leaves the
outer variable holding 10 on that path.

You can write the same computation with an immediate return in each
branch and no mutable local. Test both forms at the top end of the
signed integer range with the flag false. The decrement is still
representable, so both should return the input minus one. Computing
both candidate values before testing the flag would instead attempt
the overflowing increment. This is why lifting an expression out of
a branch requires reasoning about more than whether its operands
change. A calculation that can terminate the program on some inputs
is observable even when neither its result nor any assignment from
it reaches the return.

